WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.478
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:09.153
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.453 --> 00:00:16.238
Unit seventeen: Elliptical Constructions. Today you will avoid repetition by omitting understood words.

4
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:19.074
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.673
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.611
Ellipsis.

7
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:29.696
Ellipsis means leaving out words that are understood.

8
00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:32.190
Ellipsis avoids repetition.

9
00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:35.366
Your turn. Say: Ellipsis.

10
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.352
Ellipsis.

11
00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:41.071
Omission.

12
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:44.245
Omission means leaving something out.

13
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.257
An omission of the verb.

14
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.375
Your turn. Say: Omission.

15
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:53.200
Omission.

16
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.137
Implied meaning.

17
00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.974
Implied meaning means meaning that is understood.

18
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:01.525
The implied meaning is clear.

19
00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:04.397
Your turn. Say: Implied meaning.

20
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.594
Implied meaning.

21
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.711
So does she.

22
00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:14.436
So does she means a short reply showing agreement.

23
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.491
I like tea, and so does she.

24
00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:20.712
Your turn. Say: So does she.

25
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:24.684
So does she.

26
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.544
I think so.

27
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.711
I think so means a short reply replacing a clause.

28
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:33.481
Is it ready? I think so.

29
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:36.608
Your turn. Say: I think so.

30
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:40.503
I think so.

31
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.739
Reduced clause.

32
00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.620
Reduced clause means a clause with words omitted.

33
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:48.717
A reduced clause.

34
00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:52.282
Your turn. Say: Reduced clause.

35
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:56.483
Reduced clause.

36
00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.798
Economy of language.

37
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:02.816
Economy of language means using few words well.

38
00:02:03.417 --> 00:02:05.021
Economy of language.

39
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:08.873
Your turn. Say: Economy of language.

40
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:13.419
Economy of language.

41
00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:15.626
Context-dependent.

42
00:02:16.617 --> 00:02:20.218
Context-dependent means needing the situation to be understood.

43
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:22.886
A context-dependent phrase.

44
00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:26.867
Your turn. Say: Context-dependent.

45
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:31.350
Context-dependent.

46
00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:35.796
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.924
Visal, why do we say I can't swim, but she can instead of but she can swim?

48
00:02:43.017 --> 00:02:53.950
That is ellipsis. We omit words the listener already understands. The auxiliary can stands for can swim, which keeps speech natural and fast.

49
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.678
What are common patterns?

50
00:02:57.783 --> 00:03:12.283
So + auxiliary + subject: I like tea, and so does she. Neither does he. Substitution with so: I think so, I hope not. Also, to-infinitive without the verb: I'd love to.

51
00:03:13.383 --> 00:03:14.547
What is the risk?

52
00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:27.523
Ambiguity. Ellipsis works only if the omitted words are recoverable from context. In formal writing, check that the reader can easily restore them.

53
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:30.190
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:36.254
Grammar focus: Ellipsis: auxiliary and so and to stands for the omitted words.

55
00:03:36.554 --> 00:03:37.776
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.483 --> 00:03:40.692
I like tea, and so does she.

57
00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:43.594
I can't swim, but she can.

58
00:03:44.483 --> 00:03:46.578
Are they coming? I think so.

59
00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:54.818
Omit words that context makes clear. Use auxiliaries, so / neither + auxiliary, and to-infinitives without the verb.

60
00:03:55.583 --> 00:03:59.664
Be careful! Don't say: I like tea, and so she does.

61
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.994
Say: I like tea, and so does she.

62
00:04:04.294 --> 00:04:05.679
Invert after so.

63
00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:09.607
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.657
Saying It Short.

65
00:04:11.957 --> 00:04:14.246
I haven't finished, but my brother has.

66
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:17.314
She likes jazz, and so do I.

67
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.523
Will it rain? I hope not.

68
00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:23.803
Would you like to come? I'd love to.

69
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.413
Ellipsis keeps speech natural and avoids repeating words.

70
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.374
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:34.217 --> 00:04:36.228
I like tea, and so blank she.

72
00:04:36.528 --> 00:04:39.450
A, does. B, is. C, has.

73
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.686
The answer is A: does.

74
00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:47.594
He can't dance, but she blank.

75
00:04:47.894 --> 00:04:50.839
A, is. B, can. C, does.

76
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.133
The answer is B: can.

77
00:04:57.283 --> 00:04:59.648
'Is it ready?' 'I think blank.'

78
00:04:59.948 --> 00:05:02.870
A, so. B, it. C, that.

79
00:05:06.433 --> 00:05:08.328
The answer is A: so.

80
00:05:09.483 --> 00:05:11.924
Ellipsis is risky when the meaning is blank.

81
00:05:12.224 --> 00:05:15.657
A, short. B, unclear. C, simple.

82
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.014
The answer is B: unclear.

83
00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.369
According to the reading, what would the speaker love to do?

84
00:05:25.669 --> 00:05:29.252
A, come. B, leave. C, stay.

85
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.521
The answer is A: come.

86
00:05:35.733 --> 00:05:39.198
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.338
Write a sentence that uses ellipsis to avoid repetition.

88
00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.569
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:53.869 --> 00:06:08.575
My sister likes football, and so do I. I haven't seen that film, but my friend has. Ellipsis avoids repeating the full verb, so the sentences are shorter and sound natural.

90
00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:10.573
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:11.483 --> 00:06:17.979
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and ellipsis: auxiliary and so and to stands for the omitted words.

92
00:06:18.279 --> 00:06:23.392
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:23.692 --> 00:06:26.391
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:26.691 --> 00:06:29.808
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

